5parrow <mail.gatech@gtg692n.edu> writes:
> Jukka Lahtinen wrote:
> > (BTW, does "gener" itself actually really mean something, beside
> > being part of "generic" and "general" and some other words? Can't
> > find it in my English-Finnish dictionary but I know there may be
> > words it doesn't know..)
> 
> AFAIK it's a Latin word-fragment (not a word by itself) which means
> "to be created" or "to create" - hence "generator" (creates
> electricity), "sui generis" (self-created), etc.

No, 'sui generis' means "of its own type" (ie, not of the same type as
anything else, or "one of a kind"). 'generator' is from the verb
'genero', "to produce", which is closely related to 'genus' in its
sense of "birth" or "race" (and to 'gens', "race" or "family").

